# Code owners.
#
# On a one-person repository a single owner looks like ceremony, and for pull requests that
# a human opens, it is. It earns its place because agent-authored pull requests are opened
# by `claude[bot]`, not by a human: paired with a branch rule requiring code-owner review,
# the agent cannot merge its own work. That is a gate upstream of the deploy gate, and it
# answers the first question anyone asks about an autonomous pipeline.
#
# The second job of this file is documentation. The paths listed below are the governance
# surface of this repository — the files that decide what runs without a human. Writing them
# out makes that surface explicit rather than implied, and matches what
# `.claude/hooks/protect-guardrails.mjs` refuses to let an agent touch at all.
#
# Both mechanisms guard the same paths, at different layers and against different failures:
#   - the hook stops an agent from editing them, mid-session, deterministically
#   - CODEOWNERS stops a change to them being merged without a named human approving
#
# Note that this file is itself in the list. A governance surface that can be quietly
# widened is not one.

# Default owner for everything not matched below.
*                           @bigarobas

# --- The deterministic layer: changes here alter what the agent is permitted to do ---
/.claude/settings.json      @bigarobas
/.claude/hooks/             @bigarobas
/.github/workflows/         @bigarobas
/.github/CODEOWNERS         @bigarobas

# --- Deployment and monitoring policy ---
/bands.yaml                 @bigarobas

# --- Accepted decisions: superseding one should be a visible, approved act ---
/docs/sdlc/                 @bigarobas
